WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.120
A stem cell has two jobs.

2
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.240
It must stay a stem cell, and it must make cells that differentiate.

3
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:11.600
How can one division do both?

4
00:00:12.583 --> 00:00:15.203
In a symmetric division, the daughters are alike.

5
00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:19.173
Two progenitors enlarge the pool.

6
00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:21.813
Two differentiating cells spend it.

7
00:00:22.717 --> 00:00:26.117
In an asymmetric division, the daughters take different fates.

8
00:00:27.017 --> 00:00:30.237
Often one stays a progenitor, and the other moves toward a fate.

9
00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:33.167
Asymmetry can be built from inside.

10
00:00:34.147 --> 00:00:37.047
The mother gathers a protein on one side of its cortex.

11
00:00:38.117 --> 00:00:40.377
That protein is called a determinant.

12
00:00:41.057 --> 00:00:44.517
The mother lines its spindle up, so only one daughter inherits it.

13
00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:46.937
it can come from outside.

14
00:00:47.937 --> 00:00:52.177
Only one daughter stays touching a niche, cells that signal it to remain a stem

15
00:00:52.177 --> 00:00:53.177
cell.

16
00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:57.650
Our first map is a fly bristle, a small sense organ of four cells.

17
00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:03.253
neuron, a sheath cell that wraps it, and two outer cells that build the hair and

18
00:01:03.253 --> 00:01:04.253
socket.

19
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:10.603
In the classic scheme, all four come from one sensory organ precursor in three

20
00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:11.603
divisions.

21
00:01:12.083 --> 00:01:15.723
The precursor makes an outer precursor and an inner precursor.

22
00:01:16.703 --> 00:01:19.203
The outer one makes the hair cell and the socket cell.

23
00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:22.627
The inner one makes the neuron and the sheath cell.

24
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.260
At each division, one sister hears a signal called Notch, and the other does not.

25
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:30.280
Which is which?

26
00:01:30.833 --> 00:01:35.813
Our second map is a neuroblast, a stem cell of the fly's central nervous system.

27
00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.567
In most neuroblasts, each division is unequal.

28
00:01:40.487 --> 00:01:42.467
The large top daughter stays a neuroblast.

29
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.580
The small bottom daughter is a ganglion mother cell.

30
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.120
It divides once more, into two neurons or glia.

31
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.820
Division after division, the neuroblast stays one neuroblast.

32
00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.160
Each division adds one ganglion mother cell, which divides once.

33
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.920
These maps raise four puzzles.

34
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.100
How can two sisters born side by side differ?

35
00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.090
What does the inherited protein do?

36
00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.650
How does the mother put it on one side?

37
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.170
And what happens when the sorting fails?

38
00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:15.773
Here is the idea to carry.

39
00:02:15.773 --> 00:02:20.673
The mother puts a protein called Numb on one side, and aims its spindle at it.

40
00:02:21.533 --> 00:02:24.133
The daughter that inherits Numb cannot hear Notch.

41
00:02:24.753 --> 00:02:26.893
So it takes the Notch-off fate of that division.

42
00:02:27.793 --> 00:02:29.773
This holds where a Notch signal is on offer.

43
00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:35.777
One division can do both jobs, if the mother marks a side and only one daughter

44
00:02:35.777 --> 00:02:36.517
inherits it.
